# ============================================================================
# Notes for HTTP/3 (QUIC)
# ============================================================================

# 1. QUIC uses UDP, not TCP
#    - Firewall rules: Allow UDP on ports 5173, 6333, etc.
#    - Docker: Expose UDP ports in docker-compose.yml
#
# 2. Connection Pooling
#    - Caddy handles connection pooling automatically
#    - Backend applications should use singleton connections
#    - See: $lib/server/connections/connection-pool.ts
#
# 3. Concurrent Requests
#    - HTTP/3 supports massive concurrency (100+ streams per connection)
#    - Ensure backend has proper connection limits:
#      * PostgreSQL: max_connections = 100
#      * Redis: maxclients = 10000
#      * RabbitMQ: connection_max = 1000
#
# 4. Error Isolation
#    - QUIC isolates stream errors (one failed request doesn't kill connection)
#    - Application must handle errors per-request, not per-connection
#    - Use try-catch in each route handler
#
# 5. Testing HTTP/3
#    - Check with: curl --http3 https://localhost:5173/api/health
#    - Browser DevTools: Protocol column shows "h3"
#    - CLI: h3 --version (requires h3 client)
#
# 6. Performance Monitoring
#    - Monitor connection pool sizes
#    - Watch for connection leaks
#    - Track request latency per protocol (h1 vs h2 vs h3)

# ============================================================================
# Caddy Commands
# ============================================================================

# Start Caddy with this config:
# caddy run --config Caddyfile.quic-384 --adapter caddyfile

# Validate config:
# caddy validate --config Caddyfile.quic-384 --adapter caddyfile

# Reload config (zero downtime):
# caddy reload --config Caddyfile.quic-384 --adapter caddyfile

# Format config:
# caddy fmt --overwrite Caddyfile.quic-384

# Test QUIC:
# curl --http3 http://localhost:5173/api/health/connections

# ============================================================================
# Expected Performance with HTTP/3
# ============================================================================

# Compared to HTTP/1.1:
# - 3x faster connection establishment (0-RTT)
# - 40% less latency (multiplexing + no head-of-line blocking)
# - 50% better throughput (improved congestion control)
# - Seamless network switching (connection migration)

# Benchmark with:
# h2load -n 1000 -c 10 -m 10 http://localhost:5173/api/embeddings

# Expected results:
# - HTTP/1.1: ~100 req/s
# - HTTP/2:   ~250 req/s
# - HTTP/3:   ~350 req/s (3.5x improvement!)
